# Chat-size checkpoint (cap the re-send blowup)

Cursor re-sends the *entire* chat history on **every** turn, so a chat's token cost
grows ~**quadratically** with its length. Measured on this workspace: the pre-efficiency
mega-chats (400–2,386 turns) drove **93.8% of all token cost**, at ~11.4M re-sent
tokens per request. The fix is to **not let a single chat grow unbounded** — checkpoint
and continue in a fresh chat, carrying the state forward in ONE file.

This composes with `one-chat-per-issue` (new ticket → new chat) and
`ticket-context-persistence` (the `context.md` feed-me-first file). This rule adds the
*within-a-single-task* cap: even one ticket's chat should not run to hundreds of turns.

## When to checkpoint (the trigger)

Propose a checkpoint when ANY of these is true:
- the chat has run **~150+ turns**, or
- responses are visibly dragging a large history / attachments each turn, or
- you are about to switch model tier (per `task-tag-routing`) and want a clean handoff, or
- the user says "getting slow / big / expensive".

Do not checkpoint trivially-short chats, or mid-way through a single atomic edit.

## The checkpoint procedure (retain ALL data, then cut)

1. **Compress the bulky context (CCR).** Route the large accumulated blobs still needed
   (long logs, big JSON, wide search dumps, long file excerpts) through the active
   compressor's `compress` tool (`headroom_compress` — see `context-compression.mdc`).
   Keep the returned `hash` so the originals are retrievable; do **not** lose data.
2. **Write / update the handoff file** — `analysis-reports/<TICKET>/context.md` for
   ticket work (per `ticket-context-persistence`), else a scratch `context.md`. It must
   be a ≤1-screen feed-me-first note capturing everything the next chat needs:
   `GOAL / STATE (done+pending) / KEY FILES (path:line-range) / VERDICT-or-HYPOTHESIS /
   KEY EVIDENCE (with where it lives) / CCR hashes for compressed blobs / NEXT step.`
3. **Announce the cut, don't force it.** Cursor cannot open/close chats
   programmatically, so tell the user (one `AskQuestion`):
   > "This chat is ~N turns; re-send cost is climbing. I've compressed the bulky
   > context and written the handoff to `<path>`. Recommend starting a NEW chat that
   > reads only that file. `[Start new chat] [Continue here]`"
4. **In the new chat**, read that ONE `context.md` first (and `headroom_retrieve` any
   hash only when a specific original is needed) — never re-derive or re-read the whole
   history.

## Hard rules

- **Never drop data to save tokens.** Compression is reversible (CCR keeps originals via
  hash); the handoff file must let the new chat reconstruct full context. Efficiency
  never overrides correctness (`token-efficiency.mdc`, `no-ellipsis-truncation.mdc`).
- **The agent cannot auto-close or auto-open a Cursor chat** — it can only prepare the
  handoff and recommend the cut. The user performs the actual new-chat action.
- Prefer this cut at a **natural seam** (sub-task boundary), not mid-edit.
